
====================================================================================================
S1. What 'DESI comes in my favour' means, numerically
====================================================================================================
  The framework's stake in DESI is narrow and specific: it needs rho_Lambda to EVOLVE, because
  a0 = kappa c sqrt(G rho_Lambda) then forces a0 to evolve too, with no freedom. DESI's
  w0waCDM preference is exactly that measurement.
  combination                    w0       wa   1+w0+wa
  LCDM (null)                -1.000     0.00     0.000
  DR2+CMB+Pantheon+          -0.858    -0.58    -0.438
  DR2+CMB+DES-Dovekie        -0.821    -0.73    -0.551
  DR2+CMB+Union3             -0.662    -1.15    -0.812
  DR2+CMB (no SN)            -0.420    -1.75    -1.170
  PROVENANCE: VERIFIED BY FETCH 2026-07-30 against arXiv:2508.10514v7 Table 4 and
  arXiv:2503.14738. An earlier run of this script used w0=-0.752, wa=-0.86 for the DES row;
  that is the SUPERSEDED pre-Dovekie value and has been corrected to -0.821, -0.73.
  SIGNIFICANCE TREND, recorded because it runs mildly against the framework: the SN
  combinations are CONVERGING on ~3.2-3.4 sigma (Pan+ 2.8->3.2, DES 4.2->3.4, Union
  3.8->3.4; Nadathur Moriond 2026 after Popovic+2025 / Hoyt+2026). The 4.2 sigma headline
  is gone. The framework's most distinctive cosmological prediction needs this signal to
  SURVIVE, so a softening trend is a liability, not a detail.
  [OK] the LCDM null row has 1+w0+wa = 0 as it must

====================================================================================================
S2. The framework's parameter-free a0(z) -- both footings
====================================================================================================
  CANONICAL (rho_DE): a0 ~ sqrt(rho_Lambda) -> a0(z)/a0(0) = (1+z)^{1.5(1+w0+wa)} e^{-1.5 wa z/(1+z)}
  ALT (rho_total):    a0 ~ c H(z)/Z         -> a0(z)/a0(0) = E(z)/E(0)

  LCDM (null)
    z                  0.0      0.5      1.0      2.0      3.0
    a0/a0(0) can     1.000    1.000    1.000    1.000    1.000
    a0/a0(0) alt     1.000    1.322    1.790    3.032    4.566

  DR2+CMB+Pantheon+
    z                  0.0      0.5      1.0      2.0      3.0
    a0/a0(0) can     1.000    1.024    0.980    0.868    0.772
    a0/a0(0) alt     1.000    1.335    1.783    3.003    4.535

  DR2+CMB+DES-Dovekie
    z                  0.0      0.5      1.0      2.0      3.0
    a0/a0(0) can     1.000    1.030    0.975    0.837    0.723
    a0/a0(0) alt     1.000    1.338    1.781    2.997    4.530

  DR2+CMB+Union3
    z                  0.0      0.5      1.0      2.0      3.0
    a0/a0(0) can     1.000    1.085    1.018    0.829    0.674
    a0/a0(0) alt     1.000    1.367    1.797    2.996    4.524

  DR2+CMB (no SN)
    z                  0.0      0.5      1.0      2.0      3.0
    a0/a0(0) can     1.000    1.178    1.101    0.837    0.629
    a0/a0(0) alt     1.000    1.419    1.830    2.997    4.520

  Note the SHAPE on the canonical footing with DESI's numbers: a mild rise then a decline
  (the banked bump-then-decline law), NOT a monotone rise. A claim that the framework
  predicts a rising a0 is a Taylor-expansion artifact that drops wa.
  [OK] canonical a0(z) is bump-then-decline: 1.030 at z=0.5 rising above 1, 0.723 at z=3 falling below

====================================================================================================
S3. LCDM's natural scale, and the ABSORPTION FUNCTION A(z)
====================================================================================================
  LCDM has no a0. Its emergent acceleration scale comes from halo structure, whose
  characteristic value scales as sqrt(G rho_crit(z)) ~ H(z). So LCDM's UNADJUSTED
  expectation is g_dagger(z)/g_dagger(0) = E(z).
  Define the absorption function -- what crispy dark matter must supply:
      A(z) = [a0(z)/a0(0)]_framework / [E(z)]_LCDM      (dex below)
  combination              footing        z=0.5     z=1.0     z=2.0     z=3.0
  LCDM (null)              canonical     -0.121    -0.253    -0.482    -0.660
  LCDM (null)              alt            0.000     0.000     0.000     0.000
  DR2+CMB+Pantheon+        canonical     -0.115    -0.260    -0.539    -0.769
  DR2+CMB+Pantheon+        alt            0.000     0.000     0.000     0.000
  DR2+CMB+DES-Dovekie      canonical     -0.113    -0.262    -0.554    -0.797
  DR2+CMB+DES-Dovekie      alt            0.000     0.000     0.000     0.000
  DR2+CMB+Union3           canonical     -0.101    -0.247    -0.558    -0.827
  DR2+CMB+Union3           alt            0.000     0.000     0.000     0.000
  DR2+CMB (no SN)          canonical     -0.081    -0.221    -0.554    -0.857
  DR2+CMB (no SN)          alt            0.000     0.000     0.000     0.000
  (values are log10 A, i.e. dex of compensation LCDM must apply; 0.000 = nothing to absorb)
  [OK] on the canonical footing with DESI's numbers, LCDM must absorb 0.262 dex at z=1 -- a real, signed, sizeable requirement

====================================================================================================
S3b. *** HOW MUCH OF THAT ABSORPTION IS ACTUALLY DESI-DEPENDENT? Far less than it looks. ***
====================================================================================================
  This corrects the framing I started with, and it has to be said before anything else is
  claimed. Compare the canonical rows: the LCDM null (w0=-1, wa=0, so a0 is CONSTANT) needs
  0.253 dex of absorption at z=1, and DESI's evolving-DE case needs 0.262 dex. Those are
  nearly the same number. So the bulk of the requirement has NOTHING to do with DESI -- it is
  the old fact that a0 looks CONSTANT while E(z) RISES, which LCDM has been absorbing for
  years via concentration and feedback evolution, and largely successfully.

  The genuinely NEW, DESI-dependent content is only the framework's departure from a
  CONSTANT a0. That is the signal that would not exist if DESI returned w0=-1, wa=0:
  combination                  z=0.5     z=1.0     z=2.0     z=3.0
  LCDM (null)                  0.000     0.000     0.000     0.000
  DR2+CMB+Pantheon+            0.010    -0.009    -0.062    -0.112
  DR2+CMB+DES-Dovekie          0.013    -0.011    -0.077    -0.141
  DR2+CMB+Union3               0.035     0.008    -0.082    -0.171
  DR2+CMB (no SN)              0.071     0.042    -0.077    -0.202
  (dex deviation of a0(z) from CONSTANT -- this, not the 0.25 dex above, is the DESI test)

  THREE CONSEQUENCES, all against the exciting version of the story:
   1. The signal is SMALL: 0.013 dex at z=0.5 and 0.011 dex at z=1,
      rising only to 0.141 dex by z=3.
   2. It CHANGES SIGN. Bump-then-decline means a0 is slightly ABOVE its present value at low
      z and BELOW it at high z, crossing zero near z ~ 1.
   3. So z ~ 1 -- where most high-z kinematics actually exists -- has almost NO discriminating
      power for the DESI-dependent part. The test needs z >~ 2.
  [OK] the DESI-dependent signal nearly vanishes at z=1 (0.0110 dex) and only reaches 0.141 dex at z=3 -- so this is a z >~ 2 test, not a z ~ 1 test
  [OK] the DESI-dependent signal changes SIGN between z=0.5 and z=3, which is the distinctive shape but also why a single-redshift measurement cannot see it

====================================================================================================
S4. *** THE FOOTING FORK -- it decides whether this test exists at all ***
====================================================================================================
  This is the load-bearing result and it cuts against the exercise, so it goes first, not
  in a footnote:
    LCDM (null)              alt-footing absorption at z=1,2,3 = +0.0000, +0.0000, +0.0000 dex
    DR2+CMB+Pantheon+        alt-footing absorption at z=1,2,3 = +0.0000, +0.0000, +0.0000 dex
    DR2+CMB+DES-Dovekie      alt-footing absorption at z=1,2,3 = +0.0000, +0.0000, +0.0000 dex
    DR2+CMB+Union3           alt-footing absorption at z=1,2,3 = +0.0000, +0.0000, +0.0000 dex
    DR2+CMB (no SN)          alt-footing absorption at z=1,2,3 = +0.0000, +0.0000, +0.0000 dex
  [OK] on the ALT footing the absorption function is IDENTICALLY ZERO (max |log10 A| = 0.0e+00) for every (w0,wa) -- the framework and LCDM predict the SAME z-scaling and there is NO test
  READ THIS PLAINLY. On the alt footing a0 ~ cH(z)/Z tracks E(z) BY CONSTRUCTION, which is
  exactly LCDM's natural halo scaling. The two theories become indistinguishable in this
  channel and crispy dark matter never has to exist. So:
    * the ENTIRE prediction below is CONDITIONAL ON THE CANONICAL (rho_DE) FOOTING;
    * the footing choice is not a detail here, it IS the experiment;
    * anyone who prefers the alt footing owes no absorption function at all.
  This is the sharpest thing the exercise produces, and it is a constraint on the framework
  before it is a constraint on anyone else.

====================================================================================================
S5. CRISPY DARK MATTER, defined -- and the Occam ledger
====================================================================================================
  DEFINITION. Crispy dark matter = LCDM + a redshift-dependent suppression of the emergent
  acceleration scale, A(z), delivered through the two sectors that are genuinely free:
    (i)  halo concentration evolution c(M,z) beyond the standard fit, and
    (ii) baryonic feedback strength as a function of z (outflow efficiency, size growth).
  Required values on the canonical footing with DESI DR2 BAO+CMB+SN:
    z = 0.5   A(z) = 0.770  (-0.113 dex, i.e. suppress by 23%)
    z = 1.0   A(z) = 0.547  (-0.262 dex, i.e. suppress by 45%)
    z = 2.0   A(z) = 0.279  (-0.554 dex, i.e. suppress by 72%)
    z = 3.0   A(z) = 0.160  (-0.797 dex, i.e. suppress by 84%)

  PARAMETER COUNT -- the actual discriminator, since BOTH will fit:
    framework : 0 new parameters. a0(z) is fixed by (w0, wa), which DESI measures
                INDEPENDENTLY from BAO. Nothing is tuned to the RAR.
    crispy DM : >= 2 new parameters to span a monotone A(z) over 0 < z < 3 (an amplitude
                and a slope at minimum; realistically a free function).
  So the comparison is NOT about fit quality. It is about whether a parameter-free curve or
  a fitted function describes the same data. Occam ledger, in bans (log10 evidence ratio),
  for N high-z RAR normalisation measurements at per-point precision sigma dex:
      N  sigma (dex)  signal (dex)   bans for framework
  (scored on the DESI-DEPENDENT signal at z=3, 0.141 dex, per S3b -- scoring it on the
   0.25 dex E(z) term would be double-counting an accommodation LCDM already made)
      5         0.10         0.141                  1.2
      5         0.05         0.141                  7.6
     20         0.10         0.141                  7.6
     20         0.05         0.141                 33.5
     50         0.10         0.141                 20.6
     50         0.05         0.141                 85.3
  [OK] the ledger is expressed in bans so it can be scored later, not asserted rhetorically
  A ban is a factor of 10 in evidence. Note the ledger only favours the framework if the
  MEASURED a0(z) actually lands on the parameter-free curve -- if it lands anywhere else,
  the same arithmetic runs against the framework, and harder, because it has no freedom.

====================================================================================================
S6. PRE-REGISTERED SCOREBOARD (frozen 2026-07-30)
====================================================================================================
  WHAT WOULD COUNT AS ABSORPTION (recognisable as such because it is written first):
   1. New concentration-evolution fits appearing with a z-dependent normalisation whose
      amplitude matches A(z) above to within its errors, introduced to fit high-z kinematics
      rather than derived from N-body.
   2. Feedback-strength retuning that lowers the effective acceleration scale at z ~ 1-2 by
      26-55% in dex terms, presented as a resolution of a high-z RAR 'tension'.
   3. Any new free function of z in the halo-galaxy connection whose fitted shape tracks
      sqrt(rho_Lambda(z)) rather than E(z). That specific shape is the tell, because there is
      no LCDM reason for halo structure to know about the dark-energy density.

  WHAT WOULD FALSIFY THE FRAMEWORK (same document, equal weight):
   1. A measured a0(z) tracking E(z) within errors -> the canonical footing is wrong, the
      alt footing survives but predicts nothing distinctive here, and this whole channel dies.
   2. A measured a0(z) MONOTONE RISING steeply -> inconsistent with bump-then-decline on any
      (w0, wa) DESI allows; the a0-rho_Lambda link fails.
   3. DESI DR3+ converging back on w0 = -1, wa = 0 -> rho_Lambda constant -> a0 constant ->
      no evolution to predict, no absorption to demand, and the framework's most distinctive
      cosmological consequence evaporates. THE TEST IS HOSTAGE TO DESI, not to LCDM.

  HONEST SUMMARY OF WHAT THIS DOCUMENT IS. A conditional, footing-dependent forecast with a
  parameter ledger, not a discovery. Two findings run AGAINST the version I set out to write,
  and they are the most useful things in it:
   * S3b: most of the headline 0.25 dex absorption is the old constant-a0-vs-E(z) fact, which
     LCDM already absorbs. The DESI-dependent part is only ~0.13 dex at z=3, ~0 at z=1, and
     changes sign. So this is a z >~ 2 test with a small amplitude, not a dramatic near-term one.
   * S4: on the alt footing the prediction is identically empty. The framework must COMMIT to
     the canonical rho_DE footing to have any claim here -- and that commitment is testable.
  It also is NOT an accusation. If LCDM absorbs this, that is how the model is supposed to
  work; the only thing worth insisting on is that the absorption be counted as parameters
  spent, not as a prediction fulfilled.
